  "query_preview": "..Strong high pressure has developed over the Great\nBasin, with a weak low cutting off near the northern Baja coast.\nThis has created strong easterly pressure gradients, and very\ngusty east to northeast winds in the favored higher terrain and\ndownslope regions. Some of the strongest winds of the event are\nexpected a bit later this morning through early afternoon in these\nareas, including the Flagstaff region and eventually the Little\nColorado River Valley where gusts of 40-55 mph are expected.\nGradients will slowly weaken later tonight into Saturday morning,\nthough there will still be gusty winds at times through at least\nmid day Saturday.\n\nBy Saturday evening through Monday, the low off the Baja is \nforecast to weaken and move slowly northeastward, allowing for \nsome moisture to the south to get pulled in toward our area. There\nwill be a chance of showers and a few thunderstorms at times\nthrough this period, especially over higher terrain. Rainfall\ntotals through Monday look light for most areas, with some\nlocalized 0.50\" amounts in heavier showers.\n\nTuesday through Thursday - a deeper low pressure system is\nforecast to develop off the California coast by Tuesday and then\nslowly move eastward across our region through the end of the\nweek. The result will be increasing chances for showers and\nthunderstorms at times through the period. Some deeper moisture\ncould be drawn into the system toward Wednesday and Thursday. The\nlatest NBM probabilities of 0.50\" or greater rainfall from Wed AM\nto Fri AM are 50-70% along the higher terrain upslope regions,\nwith 1.00\" probabilities in the 30-50% range. Cooler temperatures\nand gusty south/southwest winds are also expected as this system\nmoves in.",
